"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","45","rail","signal_type","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with rail signal system standards. All 47 attributes have clear business value and are grounded in FRA regulations, AREMA standards, and NCDOT operational needs. The model covers identification (code, name), classification (category, control_method, authority_type), technical specifications (aspect_display_type, block_system_type, power_source_type), regulatory compliance (FRA compliance, PTC compatibility, inspection requirements), operational parameters (speed limits, track class applicability, visibility requirements), lifecycle management (effective dates, obsolescence tracking), and cost estimates. All attributes follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix), use appropriate data types, and include proper references to governing bodies. Data classification tags are applied appropriately (cost estimates marked confidential, no PII in this reference table). The attribute count of 47 is well within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs for rail signal type management. Minor uncertainty exists around whether some niche attributes (e.g., temperature ranges, environmental ratings) might be better suited for a separate equipment specification table, but they have legitimate business value for asset management and procurement decisions, so inclusion is justified."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","46","rail","track_classification","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with FRA track classification standards (49 CFR Part 213). All 44 attributes have clear business value for rail regulation and safety oversight. The model includes essential track class identifiers (class_code, class_name), critical safety parameters (speed limits, geometry tolerances, inspection frequencies), maintenance requirements, and regulatory compliance fields. Attribute names follow semantic-first principles without redundant product prefixes. Data types are appropriate (BIGINT for PK, INT for counts/frequencies, DECIMAL for measurements, BOOLEAN for flags, STRING for codes/descriptions). No PII/PHI/PCI data exists in this reference data product, so all tags are appropriately empty. All references cite relevant FRA, AREMA, AASHTO, and NCDOT standards. The attribute count (44) is within the suggested range and justified by the comprehensive nature of track safety regulation. Minor uncertainty exists around whether certain AREMA-specific attributes (like concrete tie requirements) are universally applicable across all track classes, but these are reasonable inclusions for a complete reference model. No semantic duplicates detected. All lifecycle timestamps (created_timestamp, last_modified_timestamp) represent distinct events. No calculated metrics or technical ETL fields included. Overall, this is a production-grade schema suitable for rail safety compliance and asset management."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","47","rail","grade_crossing","92","The schema is comprehensive and follows all mandatory rules. It includes 78 attributes covering identification, location, railroad details, roadway characteristics, traffic volumes, protection devices, safety metrics, inspection/maintenance history, accident history, improvement planning, and regulatory compliance. All attributes have clear business value aligned with FRA crossing inventory standards and NCDOT rail regulation needs. Naming conventions follow snake_case without redundant product prefixes (e.g., 'crossing_number' not 'grade_crossing_crossing_number'). Data types are appropriate Spark SQL types. Tags correctly classify sensitive data (only confidential tag applied to cost estimate). Value regex patterns are provided for categorical and formatted fields. Business glossary terms expand all abbreviations (FRA, AADT, ENS, MPO, RPO). References cite relevant standards (49 CFR Part 234, MUTCD Part 8, 23 USC 130, FRA Crossing Inventory). No technical/ETL attributes, no calculated metrics, no foreign keys included. The schema aligns with FRA crossing inventory structure while adding NCDOT-specific fields (ncdot_division, improvement_project_number). Minor uncertainty exists around whether all possible edge-case attributes are captured, but the core business requirements are thoroughly addressed with genuine business value for each attribute."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","48","asset","asset_type","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of asset type attributes relevant to NCDOT's asset management domain, with 52 attributes spanning identification, classification, financial management, lifecycle management, regulatory compliance, and operational characteristics. All attributes have clear business value and align with SAP PM, ATLAS, and government asset management standards. The schema correctly avoids redundant product name prefixes, uses appropriate data types, applies proper PII/classification tags (all attributes are non-sensitive operational data), and includes relevant GASB, AASHTO, FHWA, EPA, and OSHA references. Attribute names are semantic and clear. The model reflects real-world government asset management practices including federal reporting requirements (HPMS, NBI, FTA NTD), compliance flags (ADA, MUTCD, NEPA), and NCDOT-specific systems (ATLAS, SAP PM). Minor uncertainty exists around the exact SAP PM field mappings since specific module configurations weren't provided, but the attributes align with standard SAP PM asset class structures. No semantic duplicates were identified. All mandatory rules followed, including no technical/ETL fields, no calculated metrics, and comprehensive business attribute coverage."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","49","asset","warranty","92","The output provides comprehensive warranty attribute coverage aligned with NCDOT asset management needs and SAP PM/ATLAS systems. All 61 attributes have clear business value for warranty lifecycle management, claim processing, and compliance tracking. Naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without product name prefixes. Data classifications are appropriately applied (confidential for financial amounts and contact details, restricted avoided where not PII). System-of-record alignment includes SAP PM equipment numbers and ATLAS asset IDs. All rules followed including no foreign keys, no technical attributes, no calculated metrics. Minor uncertainty exists around whether some edge-case attributes like performance_bond_flag might be too specialized for general warranty management, but these are legitimate for government procurement contexts where performance bonds are common. The attribute count (61) is within suggested range and justified by the comprehensive nature of warranty management in a large public sector organization managing diverse asset types (vehicles, equipment, facilities, infrastructure)."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","50","asset","condition","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT's asset management domain, incorporating industry-standard condition rating systems (IRI, PCI, PSI, NBI) and federal reporting requirements (HPMS, GASB 34). All 45 attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The model correctly avoids redundant product name prefixes, includes appropriate PII/classification tags (none required for this reference data), uses valid Spark SQL types, and maps to relevant source systems (SAP PM, ATLAS, Agile Assets). Attribute names are semantic and clear. The schema covers identification, classification, thresholds, severity, actions, measurement indices, display properties, lifecycle dates, federal reporting, and financial factors. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all threshold attributes (iri_min/max, pci_min/max, psi_min/max) would be populated for every condition record or only for relevant asset categories, but including them provides comprehensive coverage as instructed. The model appropriately treats this as a reference/lookup table rather than a transactional entity, which is correct for condition codes. No structural errors or rule violations identified."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","51","asset","location","92","The output provides a comprehensive location schema with 75 attributes covering identification, classification, spatial reference (coordinates, LRS, milepost), facility characteristics, ownership, condition, environmental factors, security, contacts, and system integration. All attributes follow naming conventions (snake_case, no product prefix except location_id), use valid Spark SQL types, include appropriate PII/confidential tags, and align with NCDOT systems (SAP PM, ATLAS, TIMS, ArcGIS). The schema reflects real-world asset location management needs in a government transportation context. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all SAP PM-specific fields are necessary (planning_plant vs maintenance_plant could be debated), and the attribute count is on the higher end of the range, but all attributes have clear business justification for asset location tracking. No structural errors or rule violations identified."
