"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","86","environmental","permit_application","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 93 attributes covering identification, applicant details, project information, regulatory coordination, environmental impacts, mitigation, review process, fees, and compliance requirements. All attributes have genuine business value for environmental permit tracking. I avoided product name prefixes (using 'status' not 'permit_application_status'), applied appropriate PII tags for contact information, used industry-standard abbreviations (NEPA, SHPO, USACE, NCDEQ) with full expansions in glossary terms, and aligned with NCDOT's environmental compliance processes. The attributes reflect real-world permit application workflows including NEPA documentation types, Section 404 Clean Water Act requirements, endangered species considerations, historic preservation coordination, and mitigation tracking. I included both source system fields (likely from SharePoint/ProjectWise document management and web applications) and complementary business attributes. Minor uncertainty exists around the exact field names used in NCDOT's specific permit tracking systems, but the coverage is comprehensive and semantically sound for government environmental permitting processes."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","87","environmental","permit","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of environmental permit attributes for NCDOT transportation projects, including all fundamental business attributes (permit identification, status, dates, regulatory agency information, project details, impact measurements, mitigation requirements, compliance tracking, and contact information). The schema aligns with the specified regulatory frameworks (NEPA, Clean Water Act Section 404, Endangered Species Act, National Historic Preservation Act Section 106, NCDEQ standards) and NCDOT business context. All attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions (snake_case, no product prefix except permit_id). Data types are appropriate Spark SQL types, and sensitive data is properly classified (PII for contact information, confidential for business-sensitive data). One minor issue: attribute 'public_comment_period_end' uses 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' as the JSON key (line 68 of the attributes array) - this is a structural error that should be corrected. Otherwise, the model is production-ready with 95 attributes covering identification, classification, lifecycle dates, regulatory compliance, environmental impacts, mitigation, monitoring, enforcement, public participation, agency consultation, and contact management."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","88","environmental","mitigation_plan","92","The schema is comprehensive and covers all essential aspects of environmental mitigation plans for transportation projects. It includes 80 attributes spanning identification, impact assessment, mitigation actions, regulatory compliance, scheduling, monitoring, financial tracking, and coordination. All attributes have clear business value and align with NEPA, Clean Water Act Section 404, USACE guidelines, and NCDOT standards. Naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without product name prefixes. Data types are appropriate, PII/sensitive data is properly classified, and regex patterns are valid where applicable. The schema reflects real-world mitigation plan requirements including permit coordination, monitoring criteria, success metrics, and long-term management. Minor uncertainty exists around the optimal granularity for some coordination fields (3 agency slots may be limiting for complex projects) and whether additional specialized attributes for specific impact types (e.g., endangered species-specific fields) would add value, but the current coverage represents a solid, production-ready foundation that addresses 95%+ of business needs without unnecessary bloat."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","89","highway","segment","92","The output provides a comprehensive schema for the highway segment data product with 88 attributes covering identification, classification, geometric characteristics, pavement condition, traffic data, jurisdictional information, safety features, environmental designations, and operational status. All attributes follow the naming conventions (snake_case, no product prefix except for segment_id), use valid Spark SQL data types, include appropriate business glossary terms with full abbreviation expansions, and reference relevant standards (FHWA HPMS, AASHTO, MUTCD, NCGS, etc.). The schema aligns with NCDOT's operational systems (particularly ATLAS, TIMS, ArcGIS Enterprise, and Agile Assets for pavement/asset management) and reflects industry-standard HPMS reporting requirements. Data classification tags are appropriately empty for non-sensitive operational data. The attribute count (88) falls within the suggested range and provides comprehensive coverage without filler attributes. Each attribute has clear business value for highway operations, maintenance planning, performance monitoring, and regulatory reporting. Minor consideration: some attributes like pavement warranty fields may have limited applicability across all segments, but they represent legitimate business data when applicable and are not speculative."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","90","highway","lane","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT highway operations and FHWA HPMS standards. All 85 attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The model correctly avoids redundant product name prefixes (using 'width_ft' not 'lane_width_ft', 'type' not 'lane_type', etc.), includes essential lane identification and classification attributes, comprehensive pavement condition metrics (PCI, IRI, rutting), traffic performance measures (AADT, LOS, capacity), geometric design parameters, safety metrics, and asset management fields. All attributes are sourced from authoritative references (AASHTO, FHWA, MUTCD). Data types are appropriate, classifications follow PII/confidential rules correctly, and value_regex enumerations are accurate. The model reflects real-world highway lane management needs including ITS infrastructure, HOV operations, toll facilities, maintenance tracking, and regulatory compliance. Minor deduction for not explicitly mapping to a specific operational system module (though ATLAS, TIMS, and ArcGIS Enterprise were mentioned as likely sources), but the attribute coverage is comprehensive and industry-standard. No semantic duplicates, no filler attributes, and all naming conventions followed correctly."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","91","highway","pavement_section","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of pavement section attributes aligned with NCDOT highway operations and FHWA HPMS standards. All attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The schema includes fundamental identification (pavement_section_id, route_id, mileposts), physical characteristics (surface type, thickness, structure), condition metrics (IRI, PCI, PSI, distresses), traffic data (AADT, truck percentage, ESAL), treatment planning (priority, recommended treatment, cost), and administrative data (project number, contractor, funding). Attributes are properly named without product prefix redundancy (e.g., 'surface_type_code' not 'pavement_section_surface_type_code'). Data types are appropriate Spark SQL types, classifications follow the provided matrix, and references align with AASHTO, FHWA, and NCDOT standards. The model maps well to the Agile Assets Bridge and Pavement Management system mentioned in operational systems. Minor consideration: Some attributes like 'pavement_age_years' and 'remaining_service_life_years' could be considered calculated, but they are commonly stored in pavement management systems as business attributes for performance tracking, so their inclusion is justified. The attribute count (83) is within the suggested range and represents comprehensive coverage without filler. All mandatory requirements are met: no technical attributes, no calculated KPIs (stored condition metrics are raw measurements, not aggregations), semantic-first naming, proper classification tags, and no foreign key references."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","92","aviation","flight_schedule","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of flight schedule attributes aligned with aviation industry standards and FAA regulations. All attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix). The schema includes fundamental flight operations data (flight numbers, times, status), airport operations (gates, terminals, runways), airline information (codes, names, codeshare), operational metrics (delays, cancellations), and regulatory requirements (customs, international indicators). Data types are appropriate, PII classifications are correctly empty (no personal data in flight schedules), and references cite relevant FAA, IATA, and NCDOT standards. The attribute count (58) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs. Minor consideration: last_updated_timestamp could be viewed as technical metadata, but it serves legitimate business purposes for data currency verification in operational contexts, so it's retained. Overall, the schema is production-ready and comprehensive."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","93","highway","roadway_classification","92","The output provides a comprehensive attribute schema for the roadway_classification reference table with 50 attributes covering identification, FHWA functional classification standards, federal program eligibility, design parameters, performance monitoring, funding eligibility, and lifecycle management. All attributes follow naming conventions (no product name prefix), use appropriate data types, include proper FHWA/AASHTO/CFR references, and align with HPMS reporting requirements. The schema reflects genuine business value for highway operations and federal reporting. Minor uncertainty exists around the exact typical value ranges for some design parameters (AADT ranges, speed limits) which may vary by state policy, but the attributes themselves are standard and necessary. The attribute count (50) is within the suggested range and represents comprehensive coverage without filler. All PII/classification rules are followed (no PII in this reference table). No semantic duplicates exist. The schema is production-ready for a Databricks silver layer reference table."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","94","aviation","navigation_aid","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of navigation aid attributes aligned with FAA standards and NCDOT aviation domain requirements. All 59 attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The schema includes: (1) Core identification attributes (facility_identifier, facility_name, facility_type), (2) Technical specifications (frequency, channel, power_output_watts, service_range), (3) Geographic positioning (latitude, longitude, elevation_feet, magnetic_variation), (4) Operational status and monitoring (facility_status, monitoring_category, remote_monitoring_enabled), (5) Equipment lifecycle (manufacturer, model_number, installation_date, expected_service_life_years), (6) Regulatory compliance (environmental_clearance_status, historical_preservation_status, ada_compliant, cybersecurity_risk_level), (7) Financial attributes (replacement_cost_amount, annual_maintenance_cost_amount), (8) Inspection and maintenance tracking (last_inspection_date, next_scheduled_inspection_date), and (9) Airspace integration (artcc_code, approach_procedure_published, notam_accountability_code). All naming conventions follow snake_case without product name prefixes. Data types are appropriate Spark SQL types. PII/confidential classifications are correctly applied (financial amounts marked confidential, cybersecurity risk marked confidential). References align with FAA orders, CFR regulations, NEPA, ADA, NIST, and NCDOT standards. No semantic duplicates exist. The attribute count (59) falls within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs for aviation navigation aid management. Minor deduction for the inclusion of four temporal tracking attributes (data_source_system, record_effective_date, record_expiration_date, record_last_updated_timestamp) which could be considered borderline technical metadata, though they serve legitimate business audit and data lineage purposes in a government regulatory context."
