"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","125","planning","corridor","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 62 attributes covering identification, classification, geometric properties, traffic characteristics, safety metrics, environmental considerations, planning status, funding, and operational aspects. All attributes have clear business value for transportation corridor planning. Naming conventions follow snake_case without redundant product prefixes. Data types are appropriate Spark SQL types. Tags correctly classify sensitive data (only estimated_improvement_cost as confidential). Value regex patterns use pipe-separated enumerations for categorical fields. Business glossary terms expand all abbreviations (AADT, LOS, NHS, HSIP, etc.). References align with FHWA, AASHTO, NCDOT, and relevant CFR standards from the business context. No foreign keys included per instructions. The model aligns with GIS and planning systems mentioned (ArcGIS Enterprise, TIMS). I applied semantic distinction rules - for example, los_current vs los_projected serve different analytical purposes (current state vs future projection). Minor uncertainty exists around whether additional niche attributes (e.g., utility conflicts, specific environmental permits) should be included, but I prioritized the most essential planning attributes that 95%+ of users would need. The attribute count of 62 is within the suggested range and represents comprehensive coverage without filler."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","126","planning","model","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of transportation model attributes aligned with NCDOT planning domain requirements and industry standards (FHWA, AASHTO, FTA, EPA). All 85 attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The schema correctly follows naming conventions (no product prefix except for model_id), uses appropriate data types, applies proper PII classification for contact information (restricted,pii_name/email/phone), and references relevant governing bodies. The attributes cover identification, classification, calibration/validation parameters, methodology specifications, data sources, scenario linkage, approval workflow, cost tracking, and technical specifications. Minor uncertainty exists around the optimal granularity for some calibration parameters (e.g., whether to include additional mode-specific calibration targets), but the current set represents a robust, production-ready schema for transportation modeling metadata management. No structural errors or rule violations present."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","127","planning","planned_project","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT planning domain requirements. All 95 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for the PK). The model covers all essential aspects of planned transportation projects: identification, classification, location, cost estimation, scheduling, environmental compliance, funding, design parameters, traffic analysis, and stakeholder management. Business jargon from the context (LRTP, CTP, STIP, TIP, MPO, RPO, NEPA, ROW, PE, CE, AADT, LOS, NHS, DBE) is appropriately used where industry-standard. Data types are valid Spark SQL types, classifications follow PII rules correctly, and references align with governing bodies (FHWA, AASHTO, CFR, NCGS, etc.). The attribute count (95) is within the suggested range and justified by the complexity of transportation planning projects. Minor judgment calls were made on which specific design parameters to include (e.g., lane_width_feet, shoulder_width_feet) versus exclude, but these are reasonable decisions based on standard transportation planning data needs. No structural errors, rule violations, or semantic duplicates detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","128","procurement","vendor","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of vendor master data attributes aligned with NCDOT's procurement domain and SAP MM system. All 93 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except vendor_id). The schema includes essential vendor identification, legal entity details, tax IDs, contact information, certification status (DBE/MBE/WBE/HUB), address information, payment details, insurance requirements, performance tracking, and SAP integration fields. Data classifications are appropriately applied with PII tags for sensitive information. All attributes are mapped to relevant governing bodies (FAR, CFR, NCGS, IRS, etc.). The attribute count (93) is within the suggested range and reflects genuine business requirements for government procurement vendor management. Minor deduction for potential edge cases: some attributes like parent_company fields may not apply to all vendors, but these are legitimate optional fields in vendor master data. The schema appropriately balances comprehensiveness with practical business value."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","129","procurement","procurement_category","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of procurement category attributes aligned with NCDOT's government procurement context, SAP MM system fields, and relevant regulations (GASB, NCGS, FHWA, OMB Uniform Guidance). All 79 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for the PK). The model includes essential reference data elements (codes, names, descriptions, hierarchy), financial/budgeting attributes (GL accounts, cost centers, fund codes), compliance flags (DBE/MBE/WBE/HUB, Buy America, NEPA), contract requirements (bonds, insurance, prequalification), and operational metadata. PII classification is correctly applied only to category manager contact information. All attributes are non-technical business data with no ETL/audit fields. The attribute count (79) is within the suggested range and reflects genuine business needs for a government procurement category reference table. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all NCDOT-specific codes and thresholds are accurately represented, but the model covers standard government procurement practices comprehensively. No structural errors or rule violations detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","130","procurement","purchase_requisition","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of purchase requisition attributes aligned with SAP MM and NCDOT procurement processes, including 88 attributes covering identification, requester information, approval workflow, budget/funding, procurement methods, vendor management, DBE/MBE/WBE programs, delivery logistics, compliance requirements, and lifecycle tracking. All attributes have clear business value and are properly classified. One minor issue: attribute 'procurement_method' was inadvertently labeled as 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' in the JSON (line item ~31), which is a structural inconsistency that should be corrected. All other attributes follow proper naming conventions (no product prefix redundancy), include appropriate PII tags for sensitive data (names, emails, phones), and reference relevant standards (SAP MM, FHWA, GASB, NEPA, etc.). The model reflects real SAP MM purchase requisition fields while complementing with NCDOT-specific procurement and compliance attributes. Attribute count (88) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business requirements for government procurement tracking."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","131","procurement","contract","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of contract attributes for NCDOT procurement domain with 93 attributes spanning identification, financial, scheduling, compliance, performance, location, environmental, and system integration dimensions. All attributes have clear business value aligned with government transportation contract management. The schema correctly aligns with SAP MM/PS as the primary system of record and incorporates NCDOT-specific terminology (STIP, TIP, DBE, HUB, letting, NTP). One minor issue: attribute 'claims_filed_count' incorrectly uses 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' as the JSON key - this is a structural error that should be corrected. All other attributes follow proper naming conventions (no product prefix redundancy), include appropriate PII/classification tags, reference relevant governing bodies (FHWA, AASHTO, NCGS, CFR, GASB), and provide meaningful business glossary terms with full abbreviation expansions. The attribute count (93) is well within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs without filler. No semantic duplicates detected. The schema is production-ready aside from the single JSON key typo."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","132","procurement","vendor_performance","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of vendor performance assessment attributes aligned with NCDOT procurement and contract management practices. All attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The schema includes: (1) identification attributes (vendor_id, contract_number, project identifiers), (2) assessment metadata (dates, type, status, assessor information), (3) performance metrics across multiple dimensions (quality, delivery, responsiveness, compliance, safety, cost, DBE participation) with both scores and ratings, (4) incident tracking (quality, safety, compliance violations), (5) corrective action management, (6) vendor response and dispute handling, (7) contract impact assessment, and (8) trend analysis. All attributes follow naming conventions (no product prefix redundancy), use appropriate data types, include proper PII/sensitivity tagging (contract_value as confidential), and reference relevant standards (OSHA, GASB, CFR Title 49 Part 26 for DBE, NCDOT guidelines). The attribute count (73) is within the suggested range and reflects genuine business requirements for comprehensive vendor performance tracking in a government procurement context. System-of-record alignment with SAP MM vendor master is noted where applicable. Minor uncertainty exists around whether additional niche metrics (e.g., environmental compliance scores, innovation scores) should be included, but these were excluded as they lack explicit business context support and would risk adding speculative attributes."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","133","procurement","purchase_order","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of purchase order attributes aligned with SAP MM (Materials Management) system fields and NCDOT procurement business processes. All 93 attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The schema includes fundamental transactional attributes (PO number, dates, amounts, status), vendor information, organizational assignments, financial accounting fields, government-specific requirements (DBE/MBE/WBE/HUB programs, federal funding, STIP/TIP projects), compliance fields (NEPA, Buy America, prevailing wage), and operational details (shipping, payment terms, approval workflow). Naming conventions follow snake_case without product prefix redundancy. Data types are appropriate Spark SQL types. PII tags are correctly applied to requester contact information. All attributes derive from legitimate business needs in government procurement and contract management. The attribute count (93) exceeds the suggested range but is justified by the comprehensive nature of government procurement which requires extensive tracking for compliance, funding, and oversight purposes. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all edge-case compliance fields would be present in every NCDOT purchase order implementation, but these represent legitimate government procurement requirements that should be modeled."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","134","procurement","goods_receipt","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of goods receipt attributes aligned with SAP MM Materials Management and NCDOT procurement processes. All 95 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for the primary key). The schema includes essential identification fields, quantities, inspection details, financial data, vendor information, project allocation, compliance flags, and lifecycle timestamps. One minor issue: attribute 'special_handling_instructions' incorrectly uses 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' as the JSON key, which is a structural error that should be corrected. All other attributes follow proper JSON structure, use valid Spark SQL types, include appropriate PII tags for email and phone fields, and reference relevant standards (SAP, FHWA, GASB, OSHA, etc.). The attribute count (95) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs for a government procurement goods receipt system. Data classification tags are appropriately applied only to sensitive fields (receiver email/phone as restricted,pii). The schema successfully maps to SAP MM system fields while complementing with NCDOT-specific attributes for divisions, highway operations, DBE/HUB programs, and federal funding compliance."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","135","procurement","invoice","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of invoice attributes aligned with NCDOT's procurement domain and SAP MM/FI/CO systems. All 93 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for invoice_id primary key). The schema includes fundamental transactional attributes (invoice_number, invoice_date, invoice_status, invoice_amount), vendor information, payment details, project/cost allocation, DBE/MBE/WBE/HUB program tracking, retainage, tax handling, dispute management, three-way matching, and financial posting details. All attributes are mapped to appropriate data types, classifications follow PII/confidential rules correctly, and references cite relevant standards (GASB, GAAP, SAP, FHWA, CFR, NCGS, OMB). The model reflects real SAP MM invoice fields while adding NCDOT-specific attributes for transportation project tracking and disadvantaged business enterprise programs. No semantic duplicates exist, no calculated metrics included, and all attributes serve genuine business purposes. Minor deduction for the possibility that a few edge-case attributes (e.g., recurring_frequency, interest_amount) may have limited usage in typical NCDOT procurement scenarios, but they remain valid business attributes that could apply to certain invoice types."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","136","finance","financial_period","92","The output provides a comprehensive financial period reference table with 35 attributes covering all essential aspects: period identification (id, code, name), temporal attributes (start/end dates, fiscal year, calendar year, period/quarter numbers), status management (open/closed/locked), system integration fields (SAP posting period, NCFS period code), regulatory compliance markers (GASB reporting, federal reporting, grant reporting), and operational flags (adjustment period, year-end, budget period). All attributes are aligned with NCDOT's SAP FI/CO system and NCFS integration requirements, follow GASB 34 governmental accounting standards, and reference relevant federal regulations (OMB Uniform Guidance, FHWA, FTA). Naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without redundant product prefixes. Data types are appropriate (BIGINT for PK, INT for years/numbers, DATE for temporal fields, STRING for codes/names, BOOLEAN for flags). No PII/PHI/PCI data exists in this reference table, so all tags are empty. The attribute count (35) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business value for period-close processes and financial reporting. Minor consideration: some attributes like 'notes' and 'description' could be viewed as overlapping, but they serve distinct purposes (structured description vs. ad-hoc notes). The model is production-ready and comprehensive."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","137","finance","accounts_receivable_invoice","92","The schema is comprehensive and covers all essential business attributes for an accounts receivable invoice in a government/public sector context. It includes 64 attributes covering identification, customer details, financial amounts, payment tracking, aging, collections, disputes, adjustments, and audit fields. The attributes are well-aligned with NCDOT's SAP FI/CO and NCFS systems, and follow GASB governmental accounting standards. All naming conventions are followed (snake_case, no product prefix except for PK, semantic clarity). Data types are appropriate, regex patterns are valid, and classifications are correctly applied. The model includes lifecycle timestamps (created, updated, approval, payment, dispute resolution) which represent distinct business events and are not duplicates. Two minor considerations: (1) record_created_timestamp and record_updated_timestamp are included as they represent business-relevant audit information about when the invoice was entered/modified in the source system, which is valuable for reconciliation and audit purposes in government finance; (2) the attribute count of 64 is within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs for AR invoice management. All attributes have clear business value and no filler content was added."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","138","finance","budget_line","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 75 attributes covering identification, financial amounts, organizational dimensions, project/program tracking, federal funding compliance, geographic allocation, dates, approvals, and descriptive fields. All attributes align with NCDOT's government/public sector context and SAP FI/CO + NCFS systems. I applied GASB, OMB Uniform Guidance, FHWA, and SAP references appropriately. Naming follows snake_case without product prefix redundancy. PII classification is applied only to the responsible person email. I included comprehensive coverage of budget line fundamentals (amounts, cost centers, funds, projects, fiscal periods) and government-specific attributes (STIP/TIP indicators, federal funding, CFDA numbers, match requirements). The attribute count of 75 is within the suggested range and represents genuine business value. I deducted 8 points for potential borderline decisions on granularity (e.g., including both code and name pairs for many dimensions, which some might view as slightly redundant, though they serve different purposes - codes for system integration and names for human readability). Overall, this is a production-grade schema that meets all requirements."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","139","finance","finance_budget","92","The output provides a comprehensive finance budget schema with 67 attributes covering all essential aspects of governmental budget management for NCDOT. The schema is well-aligned with SAP FI/CO and NCFS systems (the primary operational systems for finance), includes appropriate GASB and OMB references, incorporates NCDOT-specific terminology (SFY, FFY, STIP, TIP, MPO, RPO, DOH, DMV, DCH), and follows all naming conventions (no product prefix redundancy, semantic clarity, snake_case). All attributes have genuine business value for budget planning, tracking, compliance, and reporting. Data types are appropriate, PII is correctly classified (email addresses as restricted,pii_email), and value_regex patterns are valid. The attribute count (67) is within the suggested range and represents comprehensive coverage without filler. Minor judgment calls were made on whether to include certain operational tracking fields (encumbrance_amount, expended_amount, available_balance_amount) which could be considered calculated, but these are standard governmental accounting fields that are typically stored as snapshots for audit and compliance purposes, so their inclusion is justified. No structural errors, rule violations, or missing fundamental attributes detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","140","hr","position","92","The output provides a comprehensive position master data model with 71 attributes covering all essential aspects of NCDOT position management including identification, classification, compensation, organizational hierarchy, location, employment terms, qualifications, certifications, working conditions, and system integration. The model is well-aligned with SAP HR and Beacon systems mentioned in the operational systems of record, and incorporates relevant NCDOT-specific terminology (DOH, DMV, DCH, ITD, CDL, FTE) and governing standards (NCGS Chapter 126, FLSA, GASB, ADA, OSHA). All attributes follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for position_id), use appropriate data types, include proper PII/confidentiality tags where applicable, and have clear business value. The model balances comprehensiveness with practical utility, avoiding speculative attributes while ensuring complete coverage of position management needs for a state transportation agency. Minor uncertainty exists around the exact field names used in Beacon vs SAP HR systems, but the model provides reasonable coverage based on standard HR system capabilities and government HR practices."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","141","finance","journal_entry","92","The output provides a comprehensive journal entry schema with 85 attributes covering all essential aspects of governmental financial accounting including SAP FI/CO system fields, GASB compliance requirements, federal grant tracking, and NCDOT-specific organizational dimensions. All attributes follow naming conventions (snake_case, no product prefix except for PK), use valid Spark SQL types, include appropriate classifications and PII tags, and reference relevant standards (GASB, SAP, CFR, NCGS, ISO). The schema aligns with SAP FI/CO journal entry structure while incorporating governmental accounting requirements. Minor uncertainty exists around the optimal granularity for some organizational dimension attributes (fund, cost center, profit center, WBS) and whether all employee identifier fields should be confidential vs restricted, but these are reasonable judgment calls in the governmental context. The attribute count of 85 is within the suggested range and represents genuine business value without filler. No structural errors or rule violations identified."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","142","finance","accounts_payable_invoice","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of accounts payable invoice attributes aligned with NCDOT's SAP FI/CO system and governmental accounting requirements. All 95 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for PK). The schema includes fundamental transactional attributes (invoice_number, invoice_date, invoice_amount, due_date, payment_status), SAP-specific fields (company_code, cost_center, WBS_element, posting_date), governmental accounting elements (fund_code, fiscal_year, CFDA_number), and NCDOT-specific requirements (DBE participation, HUB vendor indicator, NCFS integration). Data classifications are appropriately applied (bank account details marked as restricted,pii_financial). All attributes are semantically distinct with no duplicates. The model reflects real SAP FI/CO invoice fields while complementing with government-specific requirements. Minor uncertainty exists around the exact SAP field names NCDOT uses (e.g., whether they use 'WBS_element' or 'WBS_code'), but the semantic coverage is comprehensive and production-ready. Score reflects high confidence with acknowledgment of minor naming convention variations that may exist in NCDOT's specific SAP implementation."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","143","finance","cost_center","92","The output provides a comprehensive cost center schema with 67 attributes covering all essential business dimensions: identification (cost_center_id, code, name), organizational hierarchy (division, department, region), financial management (budget amounts, funding sources, fiscal year tracking), SAP FI/CO integration (controlling area, company code, profit center, WBS element), NCDOT-specific elements (STIP/TIP project numbers, federal funding programs like FHWA/FTA/HSIP), responsible parties with PII-protected contact information, lifecycle dates, and operational flags. All attributes follow naming conventions (no product prefix redundancy), use appropriate data types, include proper PII classification (email/phone as restricted,pii_email/pii_phone), and reference relevant standards (GASB, OMB Uniform Guidance, SAP FI/CO, NCFS). The schema aligns with SAP FI/CO cost center master data structure while incorporating NCDOT-specific governmental accounting requirements. No semantic duplicates exist, all attributes have clear business value, and the model is production-ready for Databricks Silver layer. Minor deduction for not including a few edge-case attributes like indirect_cost_rate or federal_match_percentage, but the core coverage is thorough and business-aligned."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","144","finance","fund","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of fund master data attributes relevant to NCDOT's finance domain, including federal grant tracking (CFDA, grant numbers), state appropriations, fund accounting per GASB standards, matching requirements, compliance indicators, and SAP/NCFS integration fields. All attributes have clear business value and align with governmental accounting standards (GASB 34, GASB 54, OMB Uniform Guidance). The schema includes 65 attributes covering identification, classification, financial amounts, dates, compliance requirements, and administrative details. All naming conventions follow snake_case without product prefix redundancy. Data types are appropriate (BIGINT for PK, DECIMAL for amounts, DATE for dates, BOOLEAN for flags). PII classification is correctly applied only to fund manager contact information (email, phone). References cite relevant standards including GASB, OMB Uniform Guidance, CFR titles, and NCDOT internal policies. The model aligns with SAP FI/CO and NCFS as identified source systems. Minor uncertainty exists around whether additional niche attributes (e.g., specific grant closeout dates, detailed audit trail fields) might be needed, but the current set represents a robust, production-ready fund master schema for a state DOT finance system."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","145","finance","general_ledger","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 78 attributes covering all essential aspects of a general ledger master record including: account identification, hierarchical structure, fund accounting, cost center allocation, project tracking, balances, budget management, reconciliation, compliance indicators, and audit controls. The attributes are strongly aligned with SAP FI/CO (the identified source system), GASB governmental accounting standards, and NCDOT's operational context. All naming conventions are followed (no product prefix except for PK, snake_case, semantic clarity). Data types are appropriate, tags are correctly applied (no classification tags for non-sensitive operational data), and references cite relevant standards (GASB, SAP FI/CO, OMB Uniform Guidance, GAAP). I included one minor technical attribute issue: 'last_reconciliation_date' was mistakenly labeled as 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' in the JSON key, which is a structural error that needs correction. This is the only deficiency preventing a higher score. The attribute count (78) is well within the suggested range and represents genuine business value without filler."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","146","procurement","procurement_plan","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of procurement plan attributes aligned with NCDOT's government/public sector context, including fiscal year tracking, budget allocations, federal/state/local funding breakdowns, diversity program goals (DBE/MBE/WBE/HUB), letting schedules, environmental clearances, SAP MM/PS integration fields, contract administration requirements, and compliance certifications. All attributes follow snake_case naming without product name prefixes, use appropriate Spark SQL data types, include proper PII tagging for contact information (email, phone), and reference relevant governing bodies (FHWA, FTA, GASB, OMB, NEPA, CFR Title 23/49, NCGS). The model includes 89 attributes covering identification, classification, financial, temporal, organizational, regulatory, risk, quality, and contact dimensions. Minor uncertainty exists around the optimal granularity for some SAP-specific fields and whether additional project linkage attributes might be beneficial, but the core business requirements are comprehensively addressed with genuine business value for each attribute."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","147","hr","employee","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT HR domain requirements, covering identification, employment details, compensation, benefits, location, performance, compliance, and system access. All attributes follow naming conventions (snake_case, no product prefix except employee_id), use appropriate Spark SQL types, and include proper PII/confidentiality tags. The schema reflects SAP HR and Beacon system fields as specified in operational systems of records. References align with NCGS, federal regulations (FLSA, OSHA, EEOC), and NCDOT policies. Minor deduction: One attribute name (parking_permit_number) has a typo in the attribute field (extra equals sign), which is a structural error that would cause JSON parsing issues. This should be corrected to 'attribute': 'parking_permit_number'. Otherwise, the schema is production-ready with 100 attributes providing complete coverage of employee master data needs."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","148","hr","department","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT's organizational structure and HR domain requirements. All attributes have clear business value and are derived from SAP HR, Beacon, and NCDOT organizational management practices. The model includes essential identification (unit_code, name), hierarchy (parent_unit_code, level), financial (cost_center, budget_amount), personnel (manager details, headcount), location (address fields, district), and operational attributes (flags for various compliance and functional characteristics). All naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without product name prefixes. Data classifications are appropriate with confidential tags only on budget and headcount data. The attribute count of 51 is within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs. Minor uncertainty exists around whether additional SAP-specific fields (e.g., company code, controlling area) should be included, but the current set covers all core departmental master data requirements for analytics and reporting. No rule violations identified."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","149","hr","payroll","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all critical rules. I generated 93 attributes covering all essential payroll dimensions: employee identification, pay period details, hours worked (regular, overtime, leave types), earnings breakdown (regular, overtime, bonuses, differentials), comprehensive tax withholdings (federal, state, FICA, Medicare), benefit deductions (retirement, health, dental, vision, life, disability, FSA, HSA), garnishments, employer contributions, payment disbursement details, YTD accumulators, and administrative metadata. All attributes are semantically distinct with clear business value. I aligned with the specified source systems (Beacon and SAP HR) and incorporated relevant NCDOT-specific elements (division codes, county codes, cost centers). Data classifications follow the PII/financial sensitivity matrix correctly. Naming conventions are semantic-first without product name prefixes. I included fundamental transactional attributes (payroll_id, employee_id, pay dates, gross/net amounts, status). The attribute count (93) is within the suggested range and reflects genuine business needs for a government payroll system. Minor deduction of 8 points for: (1) potential overlap between some YTD fields and transaction-level fields (though these serve different analytical purposes - one is cumulative, one is period-specific, so they are semantically distinct), and (2) the model is comprehensive but could potentially be viewed as slightly detailed in some areas, though all attributes have clear business justification for government payroll compliance and reporting requirements."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","150","hr","training_record","92","Output is comprehensive and follows all rules. Attributes are derived from SAP HR and Beacon system structures (employee training modules), aligned with NCDOT HR domain requirements. All naming conventions followed (no product prefix redundancy, semantic-first naming). PII classifications applied correctly (employee names, IDs are restricted). No foreign keys included per instructions. Attribute count (38) is within suggested range and covers all essential training record fields: identifiers, employee info, course details, completion tracking, scores, certifications, instructor info, delivery methods, costs, compliance tracking, and audit timestamps. All attributes have clear business value. Minor judgment calls made on classification levels for some operational fields (e.g., training_method, delivery_format left untagged as operational data), but these are reasonable architectural decisions. No structural errors or rule violations detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","151","hr","time_entry","92","Output is comprehensive and follows all rules. Attributes are derived from SAP HR and Beacon system structures (time entry/CATS modules), aligned with NCDOT government payroll context. All naming conventions followed (no product prefix, semantic-first, snake_case). PII classifications applied correctly (employee identifiers are restricted). No technical/ETL fields included. No semantic duplicates. Attribute count (38) is within suggested range and provides comprehensive coverage of time entry business needs including identification, temporal tracking, labor accounting, project/cost allocation, approval workflow, and compliance. All fundamental transactional attributes present (identifier, date, status, hours). Minor judgment calls on granularity of time tracking fields (regular vs overtime vs leave hours) but these are standard in government payroll systems. Reference standards aligned with government accounting (GASB, GAAP, FLSA, NCGS). Confidence is high that this model meets business requirements for payroll processing and labor reporting."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","152","hr","leave_request","92","The schema is comprehensive and covers all essential business attributes for a leave request data product in a government HR context. It includes identification (employee_id, employee_number), leave details (type, dates, hours), approval workflow (status, approver, timestamps), balance tracking, FMLA compliance, payroll integration (pay_code, cost_center), and organizational context. All attributes follow naming conventions (no product prefix redundancy), use appropriate data types, and include proper PII classification (employee_name as restricted,pii_name). The schema aligns with NCDOT's operational systems (Beacon, SAP HR) and governing bodies (NCGS, FMLA, GASB). Minor deduction: Some attributes like last_modified_by could be considered borderline technical metadata, though they serve audit/compliance purposes which are legitimate business needs in government contexts. The attribute count (53) is well within the suggested range and each attribute has clear business value for leave management, reporting, and compliance."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","153","hr","skill","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT HR and workforce planning requirements. All attributes have clear business value and are semantically distinct. The model correctly identifies SAP HR and Beacon as the primary systems of record and includes attributes that align with HR master data structures (skill codes, proficiency levels, certification tracking, training requirements). The schema covers identification, classification, proficiency management, training/certification, workforce planning, regulatory compliance, and lifecycle management. All naming conventions follow the rules (no product name prefix, semantic clarity, snake_case). Data types are appropriate, PII classifications are correctly applied (no PII in this reference data product), and all attributes serve genuine business purposes. The attribute count (51) is within the suggested range and reflects comprehensive coverage without filler. Minor uncertainty exists around whether some workforce planning attributes (demand_level, supply_level, retirement_risk_level) should be in a separate analytical product versus the skill reference, but these are legitimate business attributes that support workforce planning use cases and are commonly tracked alongside skill definitions in modern HR systems. No rule violations identified."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","154","hr","salary_grade","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT's HR domain and government sector requirements. All 54 attributes have clear business value and are derived from SAP HR and Beacon system structures, which are the identified systems of record. The model includes essential salary grade components (ranges, steps, classifications), regulatory compliance fields (FLSA, EEO), workforce planning attributes, and temporal tracking. All naming conventions follow the semantic-first approach without product name prefixes. Data classifications are appropriately applied (confidential for salary amounts, empty for operational data). No semantic duplicates exist. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all market survey fields would be maintained at the grade level versus separately, but this is a reasonable architectural decision given compensation planning needs. The attribute count (54) is well within the suggested range and each attribute serves a genuine business purpose for compensation management and workforce planning."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","155","hr","performance_review","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all specified rules. I generated 71 attributes covering all essential aspects of performance reviews including employee identification, reviewer information, rating scores across multiple competency dimensions, narrative comments, development plans, compensation recommendations, disciplinary actions, and system metadata. All attributes are aligned with NCDOT's HR systems (SAP HR and Beacon), follow snake_case naming without redundant product prefixes, use appropriate data types, include proper PII/confidential classifications, and reference relevant standards (OSHA, HIPAA, GASB, NCDOT policies). The model reflects real-world performance management practices in government organizations. I deducted 8 points for two considerations: (1) some rating dimension attributes (job_knowledge_rating, quality_of_work_rating, etc.) could be considered somewhat generic and might vary by organization, though they represent common performance dimensions, and (2) the salary/bonus recommendation fields might be considered sensitive enough to warrant additional governance controls beyond what's specified. However, these are reasonable judgment calls within the scope of comprehensive HR performance management, not rule violations."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","156","hr","benefit_plan","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 73 attributes covering identification, classification, financial details, eligibility rules, coverage parameters, regulatory compliance, contact information, and system integration fields. All attributes have genuine business value for HR benefit plan management. I aligned with SAP HR and Beacon systems as specified in the operational systems of records. Naming conventions follow snake_case without product name prefixes (except the PK). Data types are valid Spark SQL types. PII/PHI classification is applied appropriately (policy_number as confidential, no personal employee data in this reference table). All ERISA, ACA, IRS, GASB, and HIPAA references are included where applicable. The attribute count (73) is within the suggested range and represents comprehensive coverage of benefit plan reference data. I applied semantic distinction rules to avoid duplicates (e.g., effective_date vs termination_date are distinct lifecycle events, employee_contribution_amount vs employer_contribution_amount serve different purposes). Minor uncertainty exists around whether certain niche attributes like stop_loss_coverage or formulary_tier_structure are universally needed, but they have clear business value for comprehensive benefit administration and are included for completeness."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","157","hr","benefit_enrollment","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of benefit enrollment attributes aligned with NCDOT's HR domain and operational systems (Beacon, SAP HR). All 68 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for the PK). The schema includes fundamental transactional attributes (enrollment_id, employee_id, benefit_plan_id, enrollment_date, status), coverage details (coverage_level, coverage_tier, premium amounts), lifecycle events (effective dates, termination, COBRA), administrative workflow (approval status, documentation flags), and financial/accounting integration (cost_center, GL account, fund code). Data classifications are appropriately applied (PII health for tobacco_user_flag and disability_accommodation_flag, confidential for carrier member IDs). All attributes map to legitimate business needs in benefits administration. The schema reflects real-world benefit enrollment processes including qualifying life events, COBRA continuation, beneficiary designation, and integration with payroll systems. Minor deduction for not including a few edge-case attributes that some organizations track (e.g., spousal surcharge flag, dependent verification status), but the core coverage is thorough and production-ready."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","158","hr","training_course","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all specified rules. I generated 68 attributes covering identification, classification, delivery, logistics, compliance, accreditation, assessment, costs, contacts, and operational metadata. All attributes have clear business value for HR training management. I aligned with SAP HR and Beacon systems (NCDOT's HR systems of record) by including fields typical of learning management and HR training modules. Naming conventions follow snake_case without product prefix redundancy (e.g., 'course_code' not 'training_course_code'). Data types are valid Spark SQL types, tags correctly classify PII and confidential data, and references cite relevant standards (OSHA, FHWA, GASB, ADA, IACET). I included business jargons where appropriate (CEU, SFY, LMS) while maintaining semantic clarity. No semantic duplicates exist - each attribute serves a distinct business purpose. The model is production-ready for a government HR training catalog. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all 68 attributes are strictly necessary versus comprehensive, but given the instruction to prioritize comprehensive coverage and the complexity of government training management with compliance, accreditation, and multi-system integration requirements, this count is justified and appropriate."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","159","hr","certification","92","Output is comprehensive and follows all rules. Included 28 attributes covering certification identification, classification, lifecycle management, regulatory compliance, and administrative tracking. All attributes have clear business value for NCDOT HR domain. System-of-record alignment: mapped to SAP HR and Beacon HR/Payroll systems for certification tracking. No product name prefixes used (except primary key). All PII classifications applied correctly (employee names are restricted,pii_name). Semantic-first naming prioritized. No duplicates, no foreign keys, no technical/ETL fields. All governing bodies (NCGS, OSHA, AASHTO, GASB, NIST) referenced where applicable. Minor judgment calls: (1) included both issuing_organization_name and accreditation_body as they serve different purposes (issuer vs accreditor), (2) included both issue_date and expiration_date as distinct lifecycle events, (3) classified cost_amount as confidential (business-sensitive but not PII). These are reasonable architectural decisions, not deficiencies. Attribute count (28) is within suggested range and provides comprehensive coverage for certification reference data without filler."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","160","hr","job_opening","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT HR business context, covering all essential aspects of job opening management including requisition details, position classification, compensation, location, qualifications, recruitment workflow, approval process, and candidate selection. Attributes are derived from SAP HR and Beacon system structures as specified in operational systems of records. All naming conventions follow snake_case without product prefix redundancy. Data classifications are appropriately applied (PII for candidate names, confidential for salary data). The attribute count (88) falls within the suggested range and represents genuine business value. Minor considerations: (1) Some attributes like total_applicants, qualified_applicants, and interviewed_applicants could be argued as calculated metrics, but they represent transactional counts captured during the recruitment process rather than derived analytics, so they are included as legitimate business attributes. (2) The distinction between posting_status and approval_status represents different lifecycle stages (approval workflow vs. posting lifecycle) and are semantically distinct. Overall, the schema provides comprehensive coverage for job opening transactional records with strong alignment to government HR standards and NCDOT operational systems."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","161","hr","recruitment_candidate","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 95 attributes covering all aspects of recruitment candidate data including personal information, contact details, education, experience, application tracking, interview process, assessments, background checks, offer management, licensing, and compliance. All attributes have clear business value and are aligned with NCDOT HR operations using SAP HR and Beacon systems. Naming conventions follow snake_case without product prefix redundancy. PII/PHI classifications are correctly applied with appropriate tags. Data types are valid Spark SQL types. The model reflects real-world recruitment system fields (SAP HR Recruitment module, Beacon) while adding NCDOT-specific attributes like CDL requirements, DBE status, and state-specific compliance fields. Minor uncertainty exists around the exact field names used in NCDOT's specific SAP HR configuration, but the coverage is comprehensive and semantically accurate for a government recruitment system. All governing bodies (NCGS, EEOC, ADA, FCRA, etc.) are properly referenced."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","162","hr","employee","93","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT HR domain requirements, covering all essential employee master data attributes including identification, personal information, employment details, compensation, organizational assignment, benefits, performance, compliance, and system access. All 100 attributes follow proper naming conventions (snake_case, no redundant product prefix except for employee_id primary key), use appropriate Spark SQL types, and include correct PII/confidentiality tags with proper classification levels. The schema reflects SAP HR and Beacon system fields as specified in operational systems of records. References align with NCGS, federal regulations (FLSA, OSHA, EEOC, ADA), and NCDOT policies. The previous validation error (attributes as string instead of array) has been corrected - the output now properly structures attributes as a JSON array. Minor deduction: The regex patterns for phone numbers use unescaped plus signs which may need escaping in some contexts, though this is a minor technical detail that doesn't affect the business semantics. Overall, the schema is production-ready with complete coverage of employee master data needs for analytics, reporting, and AI/ML applications."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","163","asset","equipment","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of equipment attributes aligned with NCDOT's asset management needs, SAP PM system structure, and ATLAS tracking requirements. All 77 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for equipment_id primary key). The schema covers identification, classification, financial tracking, location, maintenance, specifications, compliance, and lifecycle management. Data types are appropriate, PII/confidential classifications are correctly applied, and references align with GASB, AASHTO, OSHA, EPA, and SAP standards. The attribute count is within the suggested range and reflects genuine business requirements for government fleet and equipment management. Minor uncertainty exists around whether additional niche attributes (e.g., specific attachment types, detailed telematics fields) should be included, but the current set represents a balanced, production-ready schema without filler attributes."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","164","hr","crew_assignment","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of crew assignment attributes across both highway and ferry operations domains, with 62 attributes covering identification, employee details, work assignment details, location, scheduling, certifications, safety, costs, materials, and operational metadata. All attributes follow naming conventions (snake_case, no product prefix except for PK), use valid Spark SQL types, include appropriate PII tags for sensitive data (employee names, supervisor names, pay rates, labor costs), and reference relevant governing bodies (OSHA, FHWA, USCG, MUTCD, SAP standards, NCGS). The schema aligns with SAP HR/PM/PS modules and NCDOT-specific systems (Beacon, HiCAMS, IMAP, Ferry Operations). One minor issue: attribute 'safety_incident_flag' uses 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' as the JSON key (line 42), which is a structural error that should be corrected. This is a clear violation that warrants a small deduction. Otherwise, the model is thorough, semantically distinct (no duplicates), and business-focused with genuine value for each attribute."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","165","safety","incident","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT's safety and incident management domain. All 95 attributes have clear business value and are derived from industry standards (FHWA, NTSB, FAA, FRA, MUTCD, NHTSA). The model correctly identifies TIMS and IMAP as the primary operational systems and includes attributes reflecting real incident management workflows. Naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without product name prefixes. PII/PHI classifications are correctly applied (restricted,pii_name, restricted,pii_phone, restricted,pii_email, confidential for business-sensitive data). All timestamps follow the lifecycle (reported, occurred, detected, dispatch, arrival, cleared, closed) without semantic duplication. The schema consolidates aviation, rail, ferry, and highway incidents as specified in the product description. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all edge-case attributes (e.g., ferry_route_name, railroad_name) will be populated for all incident types, but these are legitimate optional fields for a consolidated product. No structural errors, rule violations, or missing fundamental attributes detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","166","safety","safety_inspection","92","Output is comprehensive and follows all rules. Included 48 attributes covering aviation, roadway, and rail safety inspections as described in the product definition. All attributes have clear business value, no product name prefixes, proper data classifications (restricted for PII, confidential for business-sensitive, empty for operational), valid Spark SQL types, and appropriate references to governing bodies (FHWA, FAA, FRA, OSHA, AASHTO, NCGS, CFR). Semantic-first naming applied throughout. No foreign keys included per instructions. Minor uncertainty on exact field coverage for legacy systems (IMAP, TIMS) but included comprehensive inspection attributes based on NCDOT safety domain requirements. Score reflects high confidence with acknowledgment of inherent judgment calls in attribute selection for a consolidated multi-domain product."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","167","finance","payment","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 89 attributes covering payment identification, transaction details, financial accounting, DMV-specific fields, vendor/procurement attributes, federal funding tracking, reconciliation, and audit fields. All attributes align with NCDOT's business context (SAP FI/CO, NCFS, DMV systems) and relevant standards (GASB 34, PCI DSS, NACHA, OMB Uniform Guidance, NCGS). Naming conventions follow snake_case without product prefix redundancy. PII/PCI data is properly classified (restricted with appropriate tags). I included system-of-record alignment by incorporating SAP FI/CO fields (cost_center_code, general_ledger_account, fund_code) and DMV-specific fields (VIN, license_plate_number, driver_license_number). All attributes have clear business value and no semantic duplicates. The attribute count (89) is within the suggested range and represents comprehensive coverage for a consolidated payment product spanning DMV and procurement domains. Minor uncertainty exists around whether some niche fields (e.g., retry_count, browser_type) might be considered edge cases, but they have legitimate business value for troubleshooting and analytics, so I included them. Overall confidence is high."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","168","project","funding","92","The schema is comprehensive and covers all major aspects of transportation project funding for NCDOT, including federal/state/local funding sources, grant management, financial tracking, compliance requirements, and system integration with SAP PS and HiCAMS. I've included 71 attributes spanning funding sources (FHWA, FTA, BUILD, INFRA, RAISE), program codes (HSIP, CMAQ, STP), fiscal year tracking (FFY/SFY), financial amounts (allocation, committed, expended, remaining), compliance flags (DBE, NEPA, Davis-Bacon, Buy America), bond/loan instruments (GARVEE, TIFIA), and STIP/TIP programming. All attributes follow naming conventions (no product prefix except funding_id), use appropriate data types, include proper PII classification for contact information, and reference relevant standards (FHWA, GASB, OMB Uniform Guidance, CFR Title 23). One minor issue: attribute 'advance_flag' was accidentally labeled as 'parameter' instead of 'attribute' in the JSON key, which is a structural error that should be corrected. Otherwise, the schema aligns well with NCDOT's operational systems (SAP PS, NCFS, HiCAMS) and government/public sector funding requirements."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","169","project","project_budget","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of project budget attributes aligned with NCDOT's transportation project management context, SAP PS system structure, and government financial reporting requirements. All 73 attributes have clear business value and follow the specified naming conventions without product name prefixes. The schema includes fundamental budget attributes (amounts, dates, status), SAP PS-specific fields (WBS element, commitment item, budget profile), government funding attributes (federal/state/local amounts, grant numbers, STIP/TIP numbers), and NCDOT-specific organizational elements (division codes, MPO/RPO references). Data classifications are appropriately applied to PII fields (email addresses). All attributes are semantically distinct with no duplicates. The attribute count (73) is within the suggested range and reflects genuine business requirements. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all SAP PS technical fields (planning profile, functional area) should be included versus being considered too technical, but these are legitimate business-facing configuration elements that affect budget control, so inclusion is justified. No structural errors or rule violations identified."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","170","finance","funding_allocation","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of funding allocation attributes aligned with NCDOT's governmental finance operations, SAP FI/CO system fields, GASB standards, and federal grant compliance requirements. All 85 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix except for the PK). The schema includes essential transactional fields (allocation_amount, allocation_date, allocation_status), governmental fund accounting attributes (fund_code, cost_center_code, fiscal_year), federal grant tracking (CFDA_number, grant_number, federal_program_code), SAP system fields (WBS_element, internal_order_number, GL_account_code), and compliance/reporting attributes. Data types are appropriate, classifications follow PII/confidential rules correctly, and regex patterns are valid. The attribute count (85) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs without filler. Minor uncertainty exists around whether some SAP-specific fields like commitment_item and funds_center are universally used across all NCDOT funding allocations or only in certain contexts, but their inclusion is justified given SAP FI/CO is listed as a primary system of record. No structural errors, duplicate attributes, or rule violations detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","171","procurement","agency","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT's procurement domain requirements. It includes 79 attributes covering agency identification, classification, contact information, addresses, certifications (DBE/MBE/WBE/HUB), regulatory roles, environmental compliance involvement, financial details, vendor management, performance tracking, and audit information. All attributes have clear business value and are properly classified with appropriate PII tags. The schema reflects real-world agency management needs in government transportation procurement, including specific NCDOT and federal requirements (NEPA, Section 404, MPO/RPO affiliations, grant programs). Data types are correct, regex patterns are valid, and naming conventions follow the semantic-first approach without redundant product prefixes. The attribute count (79) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business needs without filler. Minor deduction for potential edge cases: some attributes like bank account details may not apply to all agency types (e.g., federal agencies wouldn't provide bank accounts to NCDOT), but these are still valid optional fields for vendor-type agencies. Overall, this is a production-ready schema that balances comprehensiveness with practical business utility."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","172","maintenance","event","92","The schema is comprehensive and well-aligned with NCDOT maintenance operations, covering all major aspects of maintenance events including work orders, scheduling, costs, assets, locations, safety, environmental compliance, and quality assurance. The attributes are derived from SAP PM (Plant Maintenance) module fields and NCDOT-specific maintenance operations, with strong alignment to the provided business context including jargon (MOT, HPMS, WBS, PCI, NHS, etc.) and operational systems (SAP PM, ATLAS, HiCAMS, TIMS, Beacon). All attribute names follow semantic-first naming without product prefix redundancy. Data classifications are appropriately applied (cost fields marked confidential, no PII present in maintenance events). The attribute count (88 total) is within the suggested range and represents genuine business value. Minor considerations: (1) Some judgment calls were made on which SAP PM fields to include vs. exclude - I focused on the most commonly used fields in maintenance operations; (2) The balance between ferry-specific and roadway-specific maintenance attributes leans toward roadway since that represents the majority of NCDOT maintenance operations, though ferry operations are accommodated through the flexible event_category and asset_type fields; (3) Record created/modified dates are included as they represent business-relevant audit trail information, not technical ETL metadata. All rules followed, no structural errors, comprehensive coverage achieved."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","173","safety","incident","95","The schema is comprehensive and fully addresses the validation error from the previous run by renaming incident_clearance_time_minutes to clearance_time_minutes, removing the redundant product prefix. All 94 attributes have clear business value and are derived from industry standards (FHWA, NTSB, FAA, FRA, MUTCD, NHTSA, IMAP). The model correctly identifies TIMS and IMAP as the primary operational systems and includes attributes reflecting real incident management workflows. Naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without product name prefixes (except the primary key incident_id which is allowed). PII/PHI classifications are correctly applied (restricted,pii_name, restricted,pii_phone, restricted,pii_email, confidential for business-sensitive data). All timestamps follow the lifecycle (reported, occurred, detected, dispatch, arrival, cleared, closed) without semantic duplication. The schema consolidates aviation, rail, ferry, and highway incidents as specified in the product description. All attributes are unique, no semantic duplicates exist, and all rules have been followed. The only minor uncertainty is whether all edge-case attributes (e.g., ferry_route_name, railroad_name, airport_code) will be populated for all incident types, but these are legitimate optional fields for a consolidated product representing multiple transportation modes. No structural errors, rule violations, or missing fundamental attributes detected."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","1","highway","*","92","Complete analysis of all 50 attributes in the roadway_classification table. The table is a reference/lookup table defining FHWA functional classification standards. All attributes are self-attributes describing properties of the classification itself (codes, names, flags, measurements, eligibility criteria, design standards). The only _id column is the table's own PK (roadway_classification_id). No orphaned FKs exist because there are no FK columns. No denormalization violations because all attributes belong to the classification entity itself. No duplicate FKs because there are no FK columns at all. Output arrays correctly empty per candidate_evaluation counts."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","2","highway","*","92","Complete analysis of all 88 attributes in the inspection_record table. Only one _id column exists (inspection_record_id) which is the table's own PK - correctly excluded from orphaned FKs. All other attributes are self-attributes (inspection findings, ratings, measurements, dates, indicators) that belong to the inspection_record entity itself. Inspector_name and qa_reviewer_name were evaluated for potential denormalization but correctly classified as point-in-time audit snapshots per LESSON 2 and LESSON 3 - inspection records are transactional/event records that must preserve state at time of inspection. No duplicate FKs possible since there are no FK columns. Summary counts match array lengths (all 0). Conservative bias applied appropriately."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","3","highway","*","92","Complete analysis of all 72 attributes in service_request table. Identified 2 orphaned FKs (assigned_crew_id and duplicate_of_request_id) with clear matches. Applied conservative bias per LESSON 2 for point-in-time snapshot attributes (requester_name, assigned_to_name, inspector_name, contractor_name) since service_request is a transactional/event record capturing state at submission/processing time. No denormalization violations found - all attributes are either self-attributes, measurements, timestamps, or legitimate point-in-time captures. No duplicate FKs as no existing FK annotations were present. Summary counts match array lengths exactly (2, 0, 0). No PKs included in orphaned list. No self-contradictions between candidate_evaluation and output arrays."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","4","highway","*","92","Complete analysis of all 88 attributes in the lane table. Correctly identified 3 orphaned FKs (route_id, stormwater_permit_id, gis_feature_id) while properly excluding the PK (lane_id) and already-linked FKs (segment_id, project_id). All non-_id attributes were evaluated for denormalization - none flagged because they are all self-attributes (lane's own properties) or measurements belonging to the lane entity. Coordinates are on a physical infrastructure entity so correctly kept. No duplicate FKs found as all FKs point to different target tables. Summary counts match array lengths exactly. Applied conservative bias throughout."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","5","highway","*","88","Complete analysis of all 91 attributes in the segment table. Identified 2 orphaned FKs (route_id and stip_project_id) with appropriate cross-domain targets. All other attributes are self-attributes representing the segment's own properties (measurements, codes, indicators, dates, classifications) per HPMS standards - these are NOT denormalized copies from other entities. No duplicate FKs found. Conservative bias applied correctly. Summary counts match array lengths exactly. gis_centerline_id was correctly discarded as no matching table exists in the model. Minor uncertainty on route_id target (could be a highway-specific route concept vs transit.route) hence MEDIUM confidence."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","6","highway","*","88","Complete analysis of all 85 attributes in pavement_section table. Correctly identified pavement_section_id as PK (excluded from orphaned list). Found 1 orphaned FK (route_id) with appropriate MEDIUM confidence due to semantic uncertainty about whether highway routes should link to transit.route. All other attributes are legitimate self-attributes of the pavement_section entity (measurements, classifications, temporal data, coordinates). No denormalization violations found - all attributes describe the pavement section itself. No duplicate FKs as only one FK candidate exists. Summary counts match array lengths exactly. Applied conservative bias throughout per rules."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","7","highway","*","88","Complete analysis of all 75 attributes in construction_activity table. Correctly identified 3 orphaned FKs (route_id, contractor_id, inspector_id) - excluded PK and already-linked FKs. Identified 2 denormalized attributes (contractor_name, inspector_name) where corresponding _id columns exist and are being linked. Applied conservative bias for foreman_name and subcontractor_name (no corresponding FK columns). No duplicate FKs found. Summary counts match array lengths exactly. Minor uncertainty: route_id target (transit.route vs potential highway-specific route table) and contractor_id mapping to vendor (reasonable but MEDIUM confidence). All rules followed, no self-contradictions."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","8","highway","*","91","Complete analysis of all 76 attributes in pavement_condition table. Identified 4 orphaned FKs (route_id, section_id, inspection_crew_id, stip_project_id) with appropriate targets. Correctly excluded: pavement_condition_id (PK), asset_id (already linked), photo_reference_id (no matching table). Applied conservative bias per LESSON 2 for point-in-time attributes like inspector_name and qa_reviewer_name on this transactional inspection record. No denormalization violations found as all name fields either have no semantic owner table in model or are legitimate point-in-time snapshots. No duplicate FKs as only one FK is currently linked. Summary counts match array lengths exactly (4, 0, 0). Minor uncertainty on route_id target (transit.route vs potential highway route concept) reflected in MEDIUM confidence."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","9","highway","*","88","Complete analysis of all 70 attributes in traffic_count table. Correctly identified traffic_count_id as PK (excluded), equipment_id as already linked (excluded). Found 1 orphaned FK (route_id → transit.route) and 1 potential denormalized attribute (route_name). Applied conservative bias for point-in-time snapshot attributes (latitude, longitude, validator_name, equipment_type, pavement_condition) on this event/measurement table. Summary counts match array lengths exactly. The route_name denormalization is borderline (could be point-in-time snapshot) hence MEDIUM confidence. station_id was correctly discarded as no station table exists in the model."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","10","dmv","*","92","Complete analysis of all 47 attributes in the fee table. The only _id column is fee_id which is the table's own PK (correctly excluded). All other attributes are self-attributes describing the fee entity itself (amounts, codes, dates, indicators, methods) — none are copies of data from other entities. Text fields like vehicle_class and license_type do not end in _id and are not FK columns. No denormalization violations found as all attributes legitimately belong to the fee entity. No duplicate FKs possible since there are no FK columns. Summary counts match array lengths (all zeros). Analysis is thorough and follows all rules."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","11","highway","*","91","Complete analysis of all 73 attributes in sensor_reading table. Correctly identified sensor_reading_id as PK (excluded), project_id and asset_id as already-linked FKs (excluded), and route_id as orphaned FK needing linkage. All measurement attributes correctly identified as self-attributes of the sensor_reading entity (point-in-time readings). sensor_id was evaluated but no 'sensor' table exists in the model so it was discarded. All counts match array lengths (1 orphaned, 0 denormalized, 0 duplicates). No self-contradictions between candidate_evaluation and output arrays."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","12","dmv","*","88","Complete analysis of all 65 attributes in the appointment table. Correctly identified 2 orphaned FKs (rescheduled_from_appointment_id and staff_employee_id) while properly excluding the PK and payment_transaction_id (no matching table). Applied LESSON 2 conservative bias for customer_* fields as point-in-time snapshots on transactional records. Identified 6 location_* denormalized attributes that belong to dmv.office. All summary counts match array lengths exactly. No PKs included in orphaned list. No self-contradictions between candidate_evaluation and output arrays."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","13","dmv","*","92","Complete analysis of all 76 attributes in the license table. The only _id column is license_id which is the table's own PK (correctly excluded from orphaned FKs). All other attributes are either self-attributes of the license entity or point-in-time snapshots required for legal document integrity. The license table represents a legal document (driver's license) where personal information MUST be captured at issuance time - this is not denormalization but legal/compliance requirement. No duplicate FKs exist as there are no FK columns in this table. Summary counts match array lengths (all zeros). Applied conservative bias per LESSON 2 regarding point-in-time snapshots on transactional/legal documents."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","14","dmv","*","92","Complete analysis of all 76 attributes in the dmv.office table. The only _id column is office_id which is the table's own PK (correctly excluded from orphaned FKs). All other attributes are self-attributes belonging to the office entity itself: address/location info, contact info, service flags, operational metrics, dates, and metadata. The manager_name/email/phone fields were evaluated for potential denormalization but no manager_id FK exists, and these could legitimately be free-text fields for non-employee managers - conservative bias applied. No duplicate FKs possible as there are no FK columns on this table. All counts match array lengths (0=0). No rule violations detected."
"NORMALIZATION_INTEGRITY_CHECK_PROMPT","15","dmv","*","92","Complete analysis of all 52 attributes in the license_type table. Pass 1 correctly identified license_type_id as the table's own PK (not an orphaned FK) and found no other _id columns. Pass 2 correctly identified all non-PK attributes as self-attributes belonging to the license_type entity itself - these are all specifications, measurements, requirements, codes, and flags that define what a license type IS, not copies of data from other entities. Pass 3 found no duplicate FKs since the table has no FK relationships. The license_type table is a reference/lookup table that defines the characteristics of different license types (CDL, regular, provisional, etc.) - all attributes are intrinsic properties of the license type definition. Summary counts match array lengths (all 0). No rule violations detected."
