"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","2","transit","*","92","Complete analysis of all 7 products in transit domain. Identified 2 genuine new in-domain FK links (ticket→fare, ticket→rider) that eliminate silos. Properly excluded existing FKs, self-references, and links to non-existent products. Candidate_evaluation was populated before links array with exact count match (2). Identified 1 HIGH-confidence M:N relationship (route↔stop) which is the foundational transit scheduling relationship modeled in GTFS stop_times. All products now connected (no silos). Minor uncertainty: ticket.fare_amount removal is conservative since transactional records may capture point-in-time values that differ from current fare definition, but fare_type is clearly definitional and should normalize. Column removal for ticket→rider is aggressive but validated (rider table contains name, email, phone, category attributes). No rule violations, no bidirectional conflicts, no cross-domain links in output."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","3","bridge","*","92","Complete systematic evaluation of all product pairs in the bridge domain. All 5 identified links are genuine INCLUDE decisions with proper normalization (new FK attributes with redundant STRING columns removed). No self-referencing links, no bidirectional violations, no existing FK re-inclusions, no cross-domain links. Candidate_evaluation was populated before links array with exact count match (5 links). All products are connected: bridge has 5 outgoing FKs to condition_rating, condition_rating has 5 incoming FKs from bridge, inspection_type and cost_item are reference tables that will have incoming FKs from other domains (inspection events, project costs). Minor deduction: Did not add bridge → inspection_type FK because bridge doesn't semantically track 'last inspection type' or 'default inspection type' - inspection type is event-specific and would belong on an inspection_event table not visible in this domain. This is a conservative but defensible decision. No M:N relationships identified (correct - these are all reference/lookup relationships). Output is structurally sound and follows all rules."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","4","rail","*","92","Complete analysis of all 8 products in rail domain. All products now have at least one relationship (inbound or outbound), achieving zero-silo requirement. Used candidate_evaluation field properly to evaluate all potential links before populating links array. All 7 links in output are genuine INCLUDE decisions with no SKIP/OMIT contradictions. No bidirectional violations (verified no reverse FKs exist in EXISTING FK LINKS). No self-referencing links. All targets are in-domain. Delta mode respected (no existing FKs recreated). Conservative on M:N detection - no genuine M:N relationships identified in this domain (rail operations are hierarchical: operators own/operate segments, segments contain structures/crossings, trains move on segments). Minor uncertainty: track_segment.track_class and similar are STRING columns that I'm proposing to replace with FKs - the exact matching between STRING values and track_classification.class_code would need validation, but this is standard normalization practice. Columns_to_remove are validated against visible target table attributes. Score reflects thorough, rule-compliant analysis with standard normalization judgment calls."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","5","aviation","*","88","Complete analysis of all 9 products in aviation domain. All products now have at least one relationship (inbound or outbound), achieving zero-silo requirement. Identified 13 new FK relationships that normalize denormalized string codes/names to proper FK references. No bidirectional violations, no self-references, no cross-domain links, no re-inclusion of existing FKs. Used candidate_evaluation properly before populating links array - all 13 entries are genuine INCLUDE decisions. Minor uncertainty on two items: (1) runway relationships cannot be modeled as simple FKs due to runway designations not being globally unique (would need composite keys with airport context, which this model doesn't support), and (2) aircraft_id on airport_fee may have nullable semantics as some fees are aggregate charges. These are reasonable architectural judgment calls, not deficiencies. Scoring 88% to reflect the inherent complexity of aviation domain relationships and the runway composite key limitation."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","6","ferry","*","92","Complete analysis of all products and product pairs in the ferry domain. All existing FK relationships were identified and excluded from output (delta mode compliance). No new in-domain FK links are genuinely needed — the domain is already fully connected through existing relationships. The candidate_evaluation field was populated BEFORE the links array, and the links array correctly contains zero entries matching the 'links_to_include=0' count. No self-referencing links, no bidirectional violations, no cross-domain links, no re-inclusion of existing FKs. The M:N analysis identified one HIGH-confidence vessel-terminal relationship with strong business justification and 6 relationship-specific attributes. Minor deduction (8 points) for the vessel-terminal M:N being somewhat implicit in current design — while the business semantics support M:N, the current model handles this through sailing_schedule as an intermediary, and there's a judgment call about whether an explicit vessel_terminal_assignment table is needed vs. deriving the relationship from scheduled sailings. This is a reasonable architectural judgment call, not a deficiency, but acknowledging the borderline nature of this M:N recommendation."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","7","environmental","*","88","Complete analysis of all products in the environmental domain with 8 genuine in-domain FK relationships identified. All links are properly evaluated in candidate_evaluation before inclusion in the links array. No self-contradicting entries, no bidirectional violations, no self-references, and no cross-domain links. All products are connected with no silos. Minor uncertainty on permit_fee: it's a reference/lookup table with no formal FKs in this model, which is acceptable for reference data, but could be argued as siloed. However, it serves as a lookup table referenced by permit_type codes, which is a valid pattern. The permit_application.permit_number FK will be NULL during application review and populated upon approval, which is operationally realistic and documented. Column removal decisions are conservative and validated against visible target table attributes. Scoring 88% due to the permit_fee edge case and the nullable FK timing consideration, but overall this is a thorough and correct analysis."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","8","highway","*","88","Complete analysis of all 10 products in highway domain. All links in output array are genuine INCLUDE decisions from candidate_evaluation. No bidirectional violations, no self-references (except existing valid duplicate_of), no cross-domain links, no re-inclusion of existing FKs. Silo check confirms all products now connected. Minor uncertainty on two decisions: (1) whether to add lane_id FK to sensor_reading and construction_activity - decided to include for sensor_reading but not construction_activity based on typical granularity needs; (2) whether traffic_count and pavement_condition need roadway_classification FK given they already have denormalized functional_class - decided against to avoid over-normalization of high-volume transactional tables. These are reasonable judgment calls in data architecture. Columns_to_remove lists are conservative and verified against visible target table attributes. No rule violations detected."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","9","dmv","*","88","Complete systematic evaluation of all 11 products in the dmv domain. All products are now connected with no silos. Identified 13 genuine in-domain FK relationships, all marked as INCLUDE in candidate_evaluation before being added to links array. No self-referencing links, no bidirectional violations, no cross-domain links, and no re-inclusion of existing FKs. Minor deduction for: (1) Initial count discrepancy in candidate_evaluation (stated 11 but actually 13 - corrected before output), (2) One judgment call on title.issuing_office where I chose to add a new FK (issuing_office_id) rather than use the existing string field - this is a reasonable normalization decision but involves creating a new attribute. All column removals are validated against visible attributes in target tables. The M:N array is correctly empty as no genuine many-to-many relationships exist in this domain (all relationships are clear 1:N ownership or reference patterns). Output is production-ready and follows all structural requirements."
