"CYCLE_BREAKER_SMART_PROMPT","10","*","*","92","I verified every edge in edge_verification before writing decisions. All 15 cycles were analyzed individually. Cycles 1-14 all contain the edge project.project→planning.planned_project (verified in each cycle's edge list), so ONE break handles all 14. Cycle 15 was correctly identified as NOT containing that edge, and I selected a different valid edge (procurement.contract.airport_id→aviation.airport) from its actual edge list. The unique_links_to_break count of 2 is accurate (one shared break for cycles 1-14, one different break for cycle 15). No silo issues: project.project retains incoming edges from gis.feature; planning.planned_project retains outgoing edges to gis.project_boundary, rail.grade_crossing, rail.track_segment; procurement.contract retains incoming from project.project; aviation.airport retains outgoing to asset.asset. All edges proposed exist in the Available FK Links list."
"CYCLE_BREAKER_SMART_PROMPT","11","*","*","92","I verified that the edge transit.route.funding_allocation_id → finance.funding_allocation exists in ALL 14 cycles by checking each cycle's edge list in the input. The FK link is explicitly documented in the Available FK Links section with source_attribute 'funding_allocation_id'. Breaking this single edge achieves optimal efficiency (1 unique break for 14 cycles). I verified no silo is created: transit.route retains many incoming FKs (from project.project, transit.schedule, transit.trip, transit.fare, asset.location, maintenance.work_order, etc.) and the transit domain remains connected to other domains via numerous cross-domain FKs. The break targets a cross-domain convenience reference rather than a core parent-child relationship. Minor deduction: I cannot be 100% certain about all incoming FKs to transit.route since I only see edges in the provided cycles, but based on the Available FK Links list, transit.route has substantial connectivity preserved."
"CYCLE_BREAKER_SMART_PROMPT","12","*","*","92","High confidence in this solution: (1) I verified the exact edge gis.project_boundary→asset.asset (asset_id) exists in the cycle's edge list - confirmed in the input. (2) The semantic analysis correctly identifies this as a computed_reference - project boundaries referencing assets being improved is a derived/convenience relationship, not core ownership. (3) Silo checks are thorough - gis.project_boundary retains project_id and spatial_reference_id FKs, asset.asset has many incoming FKs. (4) The break follows the priority order - computed_reference FKs should be broken first. (5) Only 1 cycle exists and 1 break is proposed - no over-breaking. Minor uncertainty: The alternative join path relies on project.project as intermediary, which requires the project_id FK on project_boundary to be populated. However, this is a reasonable assumption for NCDOT's data model where project boundaries are created for specific projects."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","38","grant","*","92","Complete analysis of all 8 products in grant domain. All 10 new FK relationships identified follow proper parent-child semantics and grant lifecycle flow (applicant → application → award → disbursement/expense/compliance_check). No bidirectional violations, no self-references, no cross-domain links, no re-inclusion of existing FKs. Candidate_evaluation completed before links array with exact count match (10). All products connected - zero siloed tables. Column removal recommendations are aggressive but validated against visible target table attributes. Minor uncertainty: Some FK columns (applicant_id, grant_program_id, award_id) are not explicitly visible in source product attribute lists but are strongly implied by business semantics and denormalized string attributes (e.g., applicant_organization_name, program_name, award_number). This is standard practice in grant management systems where these core relationships must exist. Deducting 8 points for this attribute visibility limitation, though the relationships are architecturally sound and business-critical."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","39","dmv","*","92","Complete analysis of all 13 products in dmv domain. All products now have at least one relationship (inbound or outbound), achieving zero-siloed-tables requirement. Candidate_evaluation was populated before links array with all potential relationships evaluated. No self-referencing links, no bidirectional violations, no re-inclusion of existing FKs, all targets are in-domain. All 10 links in the array are genuine INCLUDE decisions with clear business justification. Column removal decisions are conservative and validated against visible target table attributes. Minor uncertainty on registration.driver_id link (owner may not always be a driver in commercial/fleet scenarios, but driver is the closest entity in this domain for individual owners) - this is a reasonable judgment call. Title.driver_id was considered but excluded from final output as title holder relationship is less direct than registration owner. No M:N relationships identified - all relationships in DMV domain are clearly hierarchical (driver→license, vehicle→registration, vehicle→title). Score reflects thorough, rule-compliant analysis with one borderline modeling decision that is defensible."
"IN_DOMAIN_LINKING_SMART_PROMPT","40","grant","*","92","Complete analysis of all 8 products in the grant domain. All 10 new FK relationships identified follow proper child→parent directionality, no bidirectional violations, no self-references, no cross-domain links, and no re-inclusion of existing FKs. Used candidate_evaluation to pre-filter all decisions before populating links array. All entries in links array are genuine INCLUDE decisions with no SKIP/OMIT reasoning. Column removal recommendations are aggressive but validated against visible attributes in target tables. Zero siloed products - all products have at least one relationship (inbound or outbound). The grant domain forms a proper hierarchy: grant_program and applicant are master entities (incoming FKs only), application is the entry point (references both masters), award is the decision (references application, program, and applicant), and disbursement/expense/compliance_check are operational records (reference award and applicant). No M:N relationships identified - all relationships in this domain are clear 1:N ownership patterns. Minor uncertainty on exact column names for some new FKs (applicant_id vs recipient_id) but semantic equivalence is clear and follows FK naming rules (ends with target PK). Deducting 8 points for not having 100% visibility into whether some removed columns might have execution-specific overrides that should be retained on transactional tables."
"BATCH_SEMANTIC_FK_RESOLUTION","1","*","*","82","I am confident in 82% of these resolutions. HIGH confidence resolutions (approximately 60% of total) are based on clear semantic matches, ends-with PK matching, or self-references. MEDIUM confidence resolutions (approximately 22% of total) involve cross-domain semantic reasoning where the match is plausible but not definitive (e.g., toll plazas as asset locations, MPO/RPO as stakeholders, transit shapes as GIS features). The remaining resolutions are null values where I am confident NO match exists due to external system references, GTFS-specific concepts without corresponding tables, or cross-domain mismatches. Key uncertainties: (1) Transit operator_id - I correctly rejected rail.operator due to domain mismatch, but a transit operator table may be needed. (2) Several GTFS concepts (service_id, block_id, zone_id) have no matching tables but are core transit concepts. (3) Work center, task list, and calendar references in scheduling contexts may warrant dedicated tables. (4) Toll account_id has no matching table but represents a significant business concept. Overall, I followed the 3-pass evaluation methodology rigorously and avoided the documented failure patterns (forcing system/audit columns, cross-domain mismatches, self-contradicting reasoning)."
"FIND_MISSING_FK_LINKS","1","dmv","*","95","This is a straightforward self-referential FK case. The column 'rescheduled_from_appointment_id' clearly references the same table (dmv.appointment) to track which appointment a new appointment was rescheduled from. This follows the hierarchical self-reference pattern explicitly mentioned in the rules (similar to parent_id, reversal_id patterns). The target table exists in the model, the semantic alignment is perfect, and the decision follows all rules. All columns are accounted for, summary counts match, and no rule violations occurred."
