"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","186","project","calendar","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 26 attributes (including PK) which exceeds the suggested range of 8-20, but this is justified because calendar entities in transportation project management require extensive temporal dimensions for STIP/TIP programming, fiscal year tracking, construction scheduling, and workday calculations. All attributes have clear business value for NCDOT project management. Attribute names follow semantic-first principles without product prefix redundancy (e.g., 'date' not 'calendar_date', 'year' not 'calendar_year'). Data types are valid Spark SQL types, tags are appropriately applied (no sensitive data in calendar), and regex patterns are accurate. References align with NC state statutes, federal transportation regulations, and ISO standards. The model includes NCDOT-specific attributes (STIP cycle, letting dates, board meetings, construction seasons) that reflect the operational systems context (SAP PS, HiCAMS). Minor uncertainty exists around whether 'workdays_remaining_in_month' should be included as it could be calculated, but it has legitimate business value for real-time project tracking dashboards, so I included it. All previous feedback has been addressed (none provided). No semantic duplicates exist."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","187","rail","route","92","The output is comprehensive and follows all rules. I generated 28 attributes (including PK) which exceeds the suggested range of 8-20, but this is justified because rail routes are complex infrastructure assets requiring extensive regulatory tracking under FRA oversight. All attributes have genuine business value for rail safety oversight, compliance tracking, and operational management. Naming conventions follow semantic-first principles without product name prefixes. All FRA regulatory references are accurate and specific. Classification tags are appropriately empty for non-PII operational data. The only minor consideration is whether the attribute count might be perceived as excessive, but given the regulatory complexity of rail infrastructure and the explicit instruction that comprehensive coverage is the priority, I believe this is appropriate. No structural errors, no rule violations, and all attributes serve clear business purposes aligned with NCDOT's rail regulation and safety oversight mission."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","188","project","activity","92","The output provides comprehensive coverage of transportation project activity attributes aligned with NCDOT's project management context, SAP PS, and HiCAMS systems. All 26 attributes have clear business value and follow naming conventions correctly (no product name prefix). The schema includes fundamental attributes (activity_id, code, name, description, status), classification attributes (category, type, phase), scheduling attributes (duration_days, effort_hours, is_critical_path), approval workflow attributes (requires_approval, approval_authority), financial attributes (cost_estimate_amount, funding_category, stip_eligible), and system integration attributes (sap_activity_type, hicams_activity_code). All data types are valid Spark SQL types, tags follow classification rules, and regex patterns are appropriate. The attribute count (26) exceeds the suggested range but each attribute serves a genuine business purpose in transportation project lifecycle management. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all HiCAMS-specific attributes are universally applicable across all NCDOT divisions, but inclusion is justified given the explicit mention of HiCAMS in the business context. No structural errors, rule violations, or semantic duplicates detected."
"ATTRIBUTES_SMART_WORKER_PROMPT","189","rail","block","92","The output provides a comprehensive attribute schema for the railroad block entity with 31 attributes covering identification, classification, operational control, physical characteristics, safety systems, inspection requirements, and emergency response. All attributes follow snake_case naming without redundant product prefixes, use valid Spark SQL types, include appropriate business glossary terms with full abbreviation expansions (FRA, PTC, HAZMAT, etc.), and reference relevant governing standards (FRA Parts 213, 236, GCOR, Rail Safety Improvement Act). Data classification tags are applied appropriately (confidential for emergency contact information, empty for operational data). The schema aligns with FRA regulatory requirements and NCDOT's rail safety oversight mission. Minor uncertainty exists around whether all possible edge-case attributes have been captured (e.g., specific signal aspects, interlocking details, or maintenance history fields), but the core business attributes are comprehensive and production-ready. The attribute count (31) exceeds the suggested range but is justified by the regulatory complexity of railroad block management."
